Apple began phasing out the glowing logo on MacBooks in 2015, but it brought back the feature with the new 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models with PROLED XDR displays released on Friday. On older MacBooks, the logo was illuminated by the display's backlight, but OLED displays do not have a backlight, so a new method was required.<br/>
<br/>
<a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/11/touch_and_glow"><em>Daring Fireball</em>'s John Gruber was told</a> that Apple engineered a "custom light source just to enable the logo to glow once again, without adding any additional thickness to the lid." Why? "Because it's awesome," he heard an Apple representative say.<br/>
<br/>
By default, Apple <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149248">says</a> the logo is illuminated whenever the Mac is awake. It automatically brightens or dims based on the screen's brightness level and your environment's lighting conditions detected by the computer's ambient light sensor.<br/>
<br/>
